Here's how to implement all three:

**1 & 2. Navigation guard in `AppShell.xaml.cs`** — use `OnNavigating` with `GetDeferral()`:

```csharp
protected override async void OnNavigating(ShellNavigatingEventArgs args)
{
    base.OnNavigating(args);

    if (args.Source == ShellNavigationSource.Pop &&
        Shell.Current.CurrentPage is EditProductPage page &&
        page.HasUnsavedChanges)
    {
        var deferral = args.GetDeferral();

        bool discard = await CurrentPage.DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes",
            "Discard changes and leave?",
            "Discard", "Stay");

        if (!discard)
            args.Cancel();

        deferral.Complete();
    }
}
```

**3. Custom "Cancel" back button** — set `BackButtonBehavior` on `EditProductPage.xaml`:

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             x:Class="MyApp.Views.EditProductPage">

    <Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
        <BackButtonBehavior TextOverride="Cancel"
                            Command="{Binding CancelCommand}" />
    </Shell.BackButtonBehavior>

    <!-- your form content -->
</ContentPage>
```

```csharp
// In your ViewModel
[RelayCommand]
async Task Cancel()
{
    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
}
```

Key points:
- **`GetDeferral()`** is required for async checks (the `DisplayAlert`). Without it you get race conditions.
- The guard fires for both the hardware back button and your custom "Cancel" button since both produce a `Pop` navigation.
- If you want the Cancel button to skip the guard, set a flag before navigating: check that flag in `OnNavigating` and bypass the dialog.
